1
00:00:00,310 --> 00:00:01,900
AI can write a whole app before your

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:02,800
coffee cools.

3
00:00:03,140 --> 00:00:05,030
But ask it for one diagram,

4
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:06,250
and you get this.

5
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:09,560
Boxes everywhere. Arrows crossing arrows.

6
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:11,560
Five colours shouting at once.

7
00:00:11,930 --> 00:00:14,210
Now watch the exact same diagram again.

8
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,630
Same boxes, same facts.

9
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:18,420
Except now it looks like a design studio

10
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:19,010
made it.

11
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:21,070
This is Diagram Design,

12
00:00:21,340 --> 00:00:23,820
a free, open-source skill for AI coding

13
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:26,830
agents, with over 49,000 GitHub stars.

14
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:29,220
And its trick is one every good designer

15
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:31,610
knows, and almost no AI does.

16
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:34,050
Install it once, then just ask:

17
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:36,300
draw an architecture diagram of my app.

18
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:39,300
Your agent picks one of 44 diagram types,

19
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:40,420
tells you its plan,

20
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:42,450
and writes a single HTML file.

21
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:45,190
No build step, no JavaScript.

22
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,420
One file that opens anywhere.

23
00:00:47,730 --> 00:00:48,820
Now, the trick:

24
00:00:49,130 --> 00:00:50,330
it's not what it adds.

25
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:52,350
It's what it refuses to add.

26
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:55,260
One accent colour, saved for the one thing

27
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:57,460
that matters. Hairline borders.

28
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:00,740
Zero shadows. Everything snapped to a

29
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:01,650
4-pixel grid.

30
00:01:01,930 --> 00:01:03,470
As its own rulebook says:

31
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:05,740
the highest-quality move is usually

32
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:06,440
deletion.

33
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:08,680
And it draws far more than boxes:

34
00:01:09,230 --> 00:01:12,300
flowcharts, timelines, Sankeys, Wardley

35
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:14,980
maps, even exploded 3D views of a whole

36
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:15,580
stack.

37
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:17,150
Got an old diagram?

38
00:01:17,270 --> 00:01:18,850
Hand it a draw.io,

39
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:20,980
Mermaid or Excalidraw file,

40
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:22,110
and it redraws it,

41
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:23,800
with a receipt of what it merged or

42
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:24,350
dropped.

43
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:25,890
Want your company's look?

44
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:27,040
Give it your website,

45
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:28,900
and it pulls your colours and fonts,

46
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:30,760
and checks the text stays readable.

47
00:01:31,030 --> 00:01:32,400
It works in Claude Code,

48
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:35,010
Codex, Copilot, Cursor and more,

49
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:38,080
and exports SVG or PNG for slides and

50
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:38,760
docs.

51
00:01:38,980 --> 00:01:41,360
The catch: it's only as good as the model

52
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:43,520
running it, the same prompt can lay out

53
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:45,510
differently, and you get a picture,

54
00:01:45,530 --> 00:01:47,270
not an editable canvas.

55
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,540
If your diagrams live in Git and change

56
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:51,310
every week, stick with Mermaid.

57
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:53,470
But for the one diagram that has to

58
00:01:53,490 --> 00:01:55,320
convince someone, this is it.

59
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:57,820
One command, and your next diagram looks

60
00:01:57,840 --> 00:01:59,270
like you hired a designer.

61
00:01:59,530 --> 00:02:00,730
That's today's tool.

62
00:02:00,870 --> 00:02:03,220
Subscribe, there's a new one every day.
